(EXP-84: a text invalid from its very first character, or empty, is not a
 number in any base, and a declaration from it holds 0 - the manual's own
 `"abc" as a base5 number`, since `a` is worth 10 and base 5 cannot hold it.
 The texts are variables because a literal that is not a number is refused
 at compile time. EXP-96 and EXP-97 record what the error flag does.
 Ran on vox main dfdfc7f: vox EXP-84.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   0
   0
   0
   0
)
a text called letters is "abc".
a text called 'more letters' is "zzz".
a text called blank is "".
a number called fifth is letters as a base5 number.
Print fifth.
a number called tenth is letters as a number.
Print tenth.
a number called sixteenth is 'more letters' as a hex number.
Print sixteenth.
a number called 'nothing read' is blank as a number.
Print 'nothing read'.
